From: Coram Engineering <eng@coram.ai>
Subject: [PATCH] fmp4mux: don't skip the first stream on a late GOP

c3740cd6 ("fmp4mux: Fix handling of stream with late single GOP") added
`late_gop` to the early return of drain_buffers_one_stream() so a stream whose
queued GOPs all start after the current fragment end is skipped and the *other*
streams can still be drained.

Applying that to the first stream deadlocks the muxer. The first stream decides
the chunk end PTS. When it drains nothing, drain() finds no last GOP and
evaluates `stream_after_chunk` against `chunk_start_pts + chunk_duration` -- a
later boundary than the `fragment_end_pts` used to declare the stream late -- so
the escape does not apply and drain() returns AGGREGATOR_FLOW_NEED_DATA. No
chunk is started, no fragment is started, nothing is pushed downstream, and the
input queue grows without bound. `late_gop` is cleared and re-armed by the fill
check on the next cycle, so this repeats indefinitely and the stream never
recovers.

Before c3740cd6 the early return fired only on an empty queue, so the loop
always ran and the first stream reached its dedicated fallback:

    (gop.final_end_pts || ...) && (gop.end_pts <= dequeue_end_pts
        || (gops.is_empty() && chunk_end_pts.is_none()))

whose comment describes precisely this situation ("no GOPs were dequeued yet and
this is the first stream ... would happen if no GOP ends between the last chunk
of the fragment and the fragment duration"). That fallback drained the late GOP
and output kept flowing.

Restrict the late-GOP skip to non-first streams so the first stream reaches that
fallback again. The assertion c3740cd6 guards is evaluated before this and still
sees the original `late_gop`, and is in any case already satisfied in chunk mode
via `settings.chunk_duration.is_some()`, so the panic fix is unaffected.

Reproduced on a live H.264 + AAC pipeline (fragment-duration=2s,
chunk-duration=100ms) where a GOP began 36.76us after the fragment end: 373
drains entered in 60s, 385 NEED_DATA returns, zero chunks and zero fragments
started, and over three minutes of GOPs queued and still growing while the muxer
emitted nothing.

Also trace the early return, which was the only exit producing no buffers that
logged nothing at all.
--- a/mux/fmp4/src/fmp4mux/imp.rs
+++ b/mux/fmp4/src/fmp4mux/imp.rs
@@ -1980,9 +1980,24 @@
                 || settings.manual_split
         );
 
+        // A stream whose queued GOPs all start after the current fragment end is skipped
+        // here so that the other streams can still be drained. This must not be done for
+        // the first stream: it is the one deciding the chunk end PTS, and if it drains
+        // nothing then drain() takes the AGGREGATOR_FLOW_NEED_DATA path, no chunk and no
+        // fragment is started and the muxer stops producing output while its queue grows
+        // without bound. The first stream has a dedicated fallback below that includes a
+        // GOP ending after the dequeue end PTS, which covers exactly this case.
+        let late_gop = mem::replace(&mut stream.late_gop, false);
+
         let mut gops = Vec::with_capacity(stream.queued_gops.len());
-        if stream.queued_gops.is_empty() || stream.late_gop {
-            stream.late_gop = false;
+        if stream.queued_gops.is_empty() || (late_gop && chunk_end_pts.is_some()) {
+            gst::trace!(
+                CAT,
+                obj = stream.sinkpad,
+                "Not draining anything for this stream (queued GOPs: {}, late GOP: {})",
+                stream.queued_gops.len(),
+                late_gop,
+            );
             return Ok(gops);
         }
 
